**101. Personalized Medicine** - Tailoring medical treatment to the individual characteristics of each patient, including genetic, biomarker, and phenotypic information. For instance, oncology treatments that consider specific tumor genetics to prescribe targeted therapies, resulting in better patient outcomes.

**102. Biomarkers Development** - The discovery and validation of biological markers as indicators of normal or pathological processes, or pharmacologic responses to therapeutic interventions. Examples include using blood tests to identify specific cancer types or predict responses to therapy.

**103. Health Technology Assessment (HTA)** - A systematic evaluation of the properties and effects of a health technology, including its direct and intended effects, as well as its broader impact on healthcare systems. HTA informs policy and funding decisions, ensuring that only effective and cost-efficient treatments are approved.

**104. Digital Therapeutics** - Evidence-based therapeutic interventions delivered via software to prevent, manage, or treat a medical disorder or disease. An example is a mobile app designed to help manage diabetes through real-time monitoring and personalized feedback.

**105. Real-World Evidence (RWE)** - Data collected from real-world settings, such as electronic health records and insurance claims, to provide insights into the effectiveness and safety of medical products. For instance, analyzing patient outcomes post-market to assess long-term drug efficacy.

